FILE .patterns/jsdoc-documentation.md
1:# JSDoc Documentation Patterns
3:## Purpose
5:This document is the binding JSDoc/TSDoc law for the beep-effect workspace.
6:Documentation must render clearly in IDE hovers, teach callers how to use the API,
7:and keep every published example compilable.
9:The section grammar below ports documentation semantics from Effect v4's JSDoc
10:tooling under the MIT license. This is a semantic port only; beep has no code
11:dependency on `@effect/jsdocs`.
13:## Hard requirements
15:- Every public export has a useful lead description, canonical `@category`, and
16:  `@since 0.0.0`.
17:- Value-level exports require at least one Example. Pure type-level exports require
18:  useful prose; their Example is optional.
19:- Examples compile through the docgen TypeScript gate. Never remove an Example to
20:  hide a compilation error.
21:- New and touched documentation uses titled `**Example** (Title)` sections.
22:- `@remarks` is forbidden. `@module` and `@template` are forbidden in new work.
23:- Example code uses Effect patterns and contains no `any`, type assertions,
24:  `declare` statements, empty generator bodies, or deprecated `@effect/schema`
25:  imports.
27:## Description and section grammar
29:A doc block begins with exactly one lead paragraph. The lead explains the symbol's
30:purpose or the problem it solves instead of restating its name or signature. Do not
31:put a Markdown heading or a blank-padded fragment in the lead.
33:After the lead, the following body sections are optional. When present, they appear
34:in this exact order:
36:1. `**When to use**`
37:2. `**Details**`
38:3. `**Gotchas**`
39:4. one or more `**Example** (Title)` sections
41:Rules for all sections:
43:- A section is non-empty and appears at most once, except Example sections.
44:- `**When to use**` opens with `Use to`, `Use when`, `Use as`, or `Use with`.
45:- Details and Gotchas contain only information not obvious from the signature.
46:- Every Example has a non-empty title, and titles are unique within the doc block.
47:- Every Example contains exactly one fenced `ts` block.
48:- No fenced `ts` block may appear outside an Example section.
49:- Example sections are last in the body. TSDoc tags follow the body.
51:Sections are opt-in except where an Example is required by symbol kind. Do not add
52:empty or formulaic sections merely to fill out the shape.
54:## Carrier policy
56:`**Example** (Title)` is the canonical carrier. `@example` and `@remarks` are
57:forbidden; `AGENTS.md` states that rule without qualification and the quality gate
58:enforces it.
60:Enforcement is repo-wide and is not advisory. The zero-legacy check in
61:`bun run beep quality jsdoc-ratchet` fails when any non-generated
62:`{packages,apps}/**/src/**/*.{ts,tsx}` file contains `@example` or `@remarks`.
63:Pass `--include-generated` to scan generator outputs in the same workspace
64:corpus as well (needed when proving a codegen emitter; the default non-generated
65:scope cannot prove generated compliance). The gate inspects whole files, not
66:diff hunks.
68:For inventory presence the totals ratchet still counts either a valid Example
69:section or a legacy tag. That is a scoring detail of the fail-on-growth comparison,
70:not permission to author a legacy carrier.
72:## Kind-split Example law
74:An Example is required for value-level exports:
76:- functions and exported constants;
77:- classes, including error and schema-backed classes;
78:- runtime schemas and schema helpers;
79:- services and layers;
80:- other runtime values callable or consumable by application code.
82:An Example is optional for pure type-level exports:
84:- type aliases and interfaces;
85:- namespaces;
86:- `.Encoded` companions;
87:- same-name type companions for runtime schemas.
89:Pure type-level exports still require precise prose, `@category`, and `@since`.
90:When a type-level Example genuinely teaches narrowing, inference, or composition,
91:it may be included and must obey the full Example grammar.
93:Re-export declarations are graph edges, not separate documentation subjects.
94:Document the owning declaration rather than inventing a barrel Example.
96:## Canonical templates
98:### Value-level export
131:### Pure type-level export
144:### Before and after
146:Before, the legacy block hides semantics under `@remarks` and uses the degraded
147:tag carrier:
165:After, the lead teaches purpose, semantics have a body section, and the Example is
166:titled and observable:
190:## Tag order
192:Body sections, including Examples, always precede tags. Within the tag area, use
193:this order while omitting tags that do not add information:
195:1. `@typeParam`
196:2. `@param`
197:3. `@returns`
198:4. `@throws`
199:5. `@effects`
200:6. `@precondition`, `@postcondition`, `@invariant`
201:7. `@deprecated`
202:8. `@defaultValue`
203:9. `@see`
204:10. `@public`, `@beta`, `@alpha`, `@internal`, `@experimental`
205:11. `@category`
206:12. `@since`
208:This preserves Effect's relative order for its shared trailing tags:
209:`@deprecated` -> `@defaultValue` -> `@see` -> `@category` -> `@since`.
211:### Conditional tags
213:The default is omission. Add a conditional tag only when it supplies information
214:the TypeScript signature and body sections do not communicate.
216:- `@typeParam`: a type parameter has a non-obvious constraint or semantic role.
217:- `@param`: a parameter has units, constraints, ordering, or interactions not
218:  visible in its type.
219:- `@returns`: the result has ordering, filtering, ownership, or interpretation
220:  not visible in its type.
221:- `@throws`: the symbol can throw synchronously or produce a defect outside an
222:  Effect error channel.
223:- `@effects`: the operation writes, publishes, invalidates, or performs another
224:  side effect not visible in its type.
225:- `@precondition`: the caller must establish a non-obvious condition.
226:- `@postcondition`: a non-obvious state guarantee holds after success.
227:- `@invariant`: the operation or class preserves a non-obvious property.
228:- `@deprecated`: the API has a replacement and migration path.
229:- `@see`: a related symbol helps the reader choose, compose, or understand the
230:  API.
232:`@remarks` is not a conditional tag. It is retired; use Details or Gotchas.
234:## Described links
236:Use `{@link Symbol}` inline where the reference supports the surrounding sentence.
237:Every `@see` must include a purpose phrase after its link:
244:Bare links such as `@see {@link UserName}` are forbidden. Link-target resolution is
245:deferred to a follow-on goal; authors must still choose real, documented targets.
247:Every `@deprecated` includes a `{@link}` replacement and a concise migration
248:instruction.
250:## TSDoc grammar
252:1. Do not put `{type}` blobs in `@param`, `@returns`, or `@throws`; the TypeScript
253:   signature is authoritative.
254:2. Use `@typeParam`, never `@template`.
255:3. Write `@param name - description`; the hyphen belongs only to `@param`.
256:4. Write `@returns description` and `@throws description` without a hyphen.
257:5. Use `@packageDocumentation`, never `@module`, at package entry points.
258:6. Use `@defaultValue`, not the JSDoc-era `@default` spelling.
260:## Example quality and compilation
262:Every Example must compile via the docgen TypeScript gate and demonstrate a useful,
263:observable result: a returned or decoded value, assertion, Effect execution,
264:visible output, or meaningful inferred type. A void-discarded value is a compile
265:trick, not documentation.
267:Run a bounded edit-loop check with:
273:`docgen:local` plans from `origin/main...HEAD` plus dirty worktree files, but it
274:refuses rather than narrows when a global input moved. Changing the root
275:`bun.lock`, `package.json`, `turbo.json`, `.bun-version`, `tsconfig.json`,
276:`tsconfig.base.json`, `tsconfig.packages.json`, or anything under
277:`packages/tooling/tool/docgen/` or `packages/tooling/tool/cli/src/commands/Docgen/`
278:marks the plan `full-required`, and the bare script then prints
279:`full docgen proof required` and exits non-zero. Only `--allow-full` (what the
280:hosted `quality:docgen` lane passes) or `--full` executes the repo-wide proof.
281:An explicit package selector remains a bounded edit loop even on such a branch:
287:Package selection bypasses changed-file planning and stays scoped unless `--full`
288:is explicit. The bare automatic loop is unavailable after a global input moves;
289:the package-scoped loop is still available when the operator can name the affected
290:package. The trigger list is a hand-maintained exact-path constant in
291:`packages/tooling/tool/cli/src/commands/Docgen/internal/Local.ts`, not a Turbo
292:`globalDependencies` declaration.
294:Use the full-repo `bun run docgen` only when full proof is required. The report-only
295:quality subject remains available as:
301:Never remove an Example to make compilation pass. Fix the imports, API usage, or
302:example itself.
304:### Runnable Example fences
306:Examples selected for runtime documentation testing use the canonical fence info
307:string `ts import.meta.vitest name="<Example title>"`. The name is derived from
308:the enclosing `**Example** (Title)` heading. A trailing `// => expected` comment
309:asserts the value of the expression on the same line using Effect equality. It
310:may trail an expression statement or one initialized `const` identifier. It may
311:appear inside control flow only when that control-flow body has an explicit
312:block.
314:Runnable fences complement the docgen TypeScript gate; they do not replace it.
315:Type-only fences still compile through docgen and are reported as vacuous by
316:`bun run beep docgen doctest verify`. Do not add a tautological runtime assertion
317:to make a type-only fence appear executable.
319:For pure new and touched Examples, run `bun run beep docgen doctest verify` and
320:use `bun run beep docgen doctest mark --write` to apply the canonical marker and
321:safe assertion rewrites. `console.log(expression) // expected` is cleanup-on-touch:
322:when the expected text is literal-like and the command can prove an equivalent
323:doctest assertion, rewrite it to `expression // => expected`. Keep console output
324:when the displayed value is prose, formatting, or otherwise not a valid expected
325:TypeScript expression. Never mark examples that use environment variables,
326:filesystem, network, child processes, Bun APIs, databases, external or relative
327:imports, JSX, or React.
329:## Imports inside Examples
331:Keep beep's namespace-import law; do not port Effect's named-import rule.
333:| Module | Required form |
334:| --- | --- |
335:| `effect/Schema` | `import * as S from "effect/Schema"` |
336:| `effect/Array` | `import * as A from "effect/Array"` |
337:| `effect/Option` | `import * as O from "effect/Option"` |
338:| `effect/Predicate` | `import * as P from "effect/Predicate"` |
339:| `effect/Record` | `import * as R from "effect/Record"` |
341:Core combinators may use named imports from the root `effect` module, for example
342:`import { Console, Effect, Layer } from "effect"`. Never import from the deprecated
343:`@effect/schema` package.
345:## Schema and Effect-specific guidance
347:- Schema values and schema-backed classes are value-level and require an Example.
348:- Same-name schema type aliases and `.Encoded` companions are pure type-level and
349:  require prose only.
350:- Error classes are value-level; show how a caller constructs, raises, or handles
351:  the error.
352:- Services and layers are value-level; show acquisition, provision, or a useful
353:  method call.
354:- For `Effect<A, E, R>` results, do not restate the channels in tags. Use prose for
355:  non-obvious failure or environment semantics.
356:- A `Fn` method takes its type from its `output` schema. When that output is
357:  `S.declare((u): u is Effect.Effect<A, E, R> => Effect.isEffect(u))` with a
358:  non-`never` `R`, the requirement channel
359:  travels with the method, so `Effect.runPromise(shape.method(x))` fails to
360:  typecheck even when the fixture implementation needs nothing. Either provide
361:  the requirements first — `Effect.runPromise(Effect.provide(shape.method(x), deps))`
362:  — or compose the effect without running it. A `Fn` whose output declares
363:  `R = never` runs normally.
364:- Document a `dual` operation once on the outer declaration unless its call forms
365:  have meaningfully different semantics.
367:## Category annotation
369:Use the most specific canonical kebab-case role from
370:`packages/tooling/library/repo-utils/src/schemas/JSDocCategories.ts`. Choose the
371:symbol's semantic role rather than its file or architectural layer.
373:- Core API roles: `models`, `schemas`, `type-level`, `constructors`, `factories`,
374:  `destructors`, `combinators`, `predicates`, `guards`, `refinements`,
375:  `assertions`, `getters`, `setters`, `mapping`, `filtering`, `folding`,
376:  `sequencing`, `concurrency`, `resource-management`, `error-handling`,
377:  `utilities`, `layers`.
378:- Domain roles: `aggregates`, `entities`, `value-objects`, `domain-events`,
379:  `policies`, `specifications`, `identifiers`, `entity-ids`, `type-ids`, `symbols`,
380:  `errors`.
381:- Application and ports: `use-cases`, `commands`, `queries`, `events`,
382:  `workflows`, `processes`, `schedulers`, `protocols`, `ports`, `services`,
383:  `handlers`, `endpoints`, `clients`, `adapters`, `repositories`, `projections`,
384:  `read-models`, `tables`.
385:- Data boundaries: `validation`, `parsing`, `encoding`, `decoding`,
386:  `serialization`, `codecs`, `formatting`, `normalization`, `dtos`, `mappers`.
387:- UI and client state: `components`, `hooks`, `providers`, `themes`, `tokens`,
388:  `forms`, `atoms`.
389:- Tooling and cross-cutting: `tools`, `tool-schemas`, `cli-commands`,
390:  `configuration`, `constants`, `observability`, `diagnostics`, `fixtures`,
391:  `testing`, `streams`, `resources`, `interop`.
393:Legacy aliases are accepted only for untouched documentation. New and touched blocks
394:use canonical slugs. Values such as `exports`, `re-exports`, `modules`, `core`,
395:`generated`, and `presentation` are rejected because they describe topology rather
396:than a symbol's role.
398:## Custom agent-context tags
400:The registered `@effects`, `@precondition`, `@postcondition`, and `@invariant`
401:tags capture call-site contracts that a signature cannot express. Keep them concise;
402:use Details or Gotchas for general semantics.
411:## Forbidden patterns
413:- `@remarks`, or `@module` / `@template` in new or touched documentation.
414:- An undescribed `@see`.
415:- Duplicate, empty, out-of-order, or post-Example body sections.
416:- An untitled Example, duplicate Example title, multiple fences in one Example,
417:  or a loose `ts` fence outside an Example.
418:- Type braces in tags or a hyphen after `@returns`/`@throws`.
419:- Named imports from `effect/Schema`, `effect/Array`, `effect/Option`,
420:  `effect/Predicate`, or `effect/Record`.
421:- `any`, type assertions, `declare`, empty `Effect.gen`, or non-compiling code in
422:  Examples.
423:- Removing an Example to avoid fixing it.
425:## Review checklist
427:- [ ] Lead description is one useful paragraph.
428:- [ ] Optional sections are non-empty, unique, and in canonical order.
429:- [ ] When-to-use text begins with an allowed opener.
430:- [ ] Every required value-level Example is titled, unique, last, single-fence,
431:      observable, and compilable.
432:- [ ] Pure type-level exports have useful prose; Examples are included only when
433:      pedagogically valuable.
434:- [ ] No loose `ts` fences exist in touched documentation, and no legacy carriers
435:      remain anywhere under `{packages,apps}/**/src`.
436:- [ ] Every `@see` has a purpose phrase; every deprecation links a replacement.
437:- [ ] Conditional tags add information beyond the signature and follow tag order.
438:- [ ] Imports use canonical namespace aliases.
439:- [ ] `@category` is canonical and `@since` is exactly `0.0.0`.
440:- [ ] TSDoc grammar is clean: no `{type}`, `@template`, `@module`, or misplaced
441:      hyphen.
442:- [ ] Schema annotations remain aligned with runtime schemas.
443:- [ ] `bun run docgen:local` passes for the changed documentation.
